Repair only the supplied scientific or grammatical issues. Return {sections:[{id:number,patches:[{before:string,after:string,issueIndex:number}],reason:string}]}. Include each supplied section exactly once. Use exact unique substrings in its candidate. At most 3 non-overlapping patches per section; their combined BEFORE text must cover at most 35% of that section. issueIndex refers to that section's issues. Keep wording elsewhere unchanged. Restore the original meaning, uncertainty and proposal status. No extra style pass. If bounded repairs cannot solve the issues, return patches:[] and explain.
INPUT DATA:
{"sections": [{"id": 3, "heading": "Methodology", "original": "We will compare manual and optical sorting over 20 batches [S2]. Each method will process an equal input mass from each batch. The recovered output will be separated into target material and contaminants before the measures are calculated. We will retain the batch-level records so that a high average does not hide a small number of poorly sorted batches.", "candidate": "We will compare manual and optical sorting over 20 batches [S2]. Each method will process an equal input mass from each batch. The recovered output will be separated into target material and contaminants before calculating the measures. We will retain batch-level records to ensure that a high average does not mask the performance of poorly sorted batches.", "issues": [{"originalQuote": "before the measures are calculated", "revisedQuote": "before calculating the measures", "problem": "The implied subject of 'calculating' is the recovered output, creating an awkward modifier. Retain the passive clause or explicitly identify who calculates the measures."}, {"originalQuote": "so that a high average does not hide a small number of poorly sorted batches", "revisedQuote": "to ensure that a high average does not mask the performance of poorly sorted batches", "problem": "The revision loses the explicit concern about a small number of poorly sorted batches and strengthens the wording to a guarantee, although retaining records alone does not ensure that aggregate reporting exposes those batches."}]}], "sources": [{"id": "S1", "title": "Controlled source packet: domain and evidence brief", "excerpt": "The proposed recycling study separates recovered mass from material purity. A larger recovered mass can contain more contaminants and does not necessarily imply better recovery quality. The material stream contains aluminium and plastic. No claim about lifecycle emissions follows from mass measurements alone.", "content_kind": "author_constructed_evaluation_fixture"}, {"id": "S2", "title": "Controlled source packet: experimental design brief", "excerpt": "The team will compare manual sorting with optical sorting over 20 batches. Both methods will process equal input mass from each batch. Recovery is defined relative to target material in the input; purity is defined relative to recovered output mass. The measures must remain separate.", "content_kind": "author_constructed_evaluation_fixture"}]}
